Mitral Regurgitation
Cardiovascular
Pansystolic murmur radiating to axilla, Displaced hyperdynamic apex, Soft S1, Backward systolic jet on echo

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-027
Reason for Visit: Progressive worsening of breathing at night and increasing generalized fatigue over the past two weeks.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a significant decline in the patient's functional status, marked by new orthopnea and severe exertional fatigue. This presentation strongly suggests acute decompensation of a structural heart valve issue leading to heart failure. Urgent assessment was necessary to evaluate left ventricular function, identify signs of pulmonary edema, and prevent severe respiratory failure before the next routine clinical availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I can't walk to the kitchen without getting winded, and I have to prop myself up with extra pillows just to sleep."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports progressive exertional dyspnea and severe fatigue developing over the last 3-4 months, which has acutely worsened over the last two weeks. Now experiencing orthopnea (requiring 3 pillows) and occasional nocturnal cough. Denies chest pain or palpitations. Notes mild swelling in the ankles by the end of the day. KEY SYMPTOMS: Exertional dyspnoea and fatigue. Orthopnoea in severe or chronic cases. Pansystolic high-pitched blowing murmur (noted as newly symptomatic).
Functional Status: Declining; currently struggling with basic activities of daily living (ADLs).
Social/Safety Concerns: Spouse is anxious about the patient's rapidly declining stamina and inability to lie flat.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 20 | SpO2: 94 percent | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Fatigued, mildly tachypneic at rest, comfortable when sitting upright.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Laterally displaced and hyperdynamic, thrusting apex beat. Soft first heart sound (S1) and widely split second heart sound (S2). A loud, high-pitched pansystolic blowing murmur is audible, loudest at the cardiac apex, radiating clearly to the left axilla. A prominent third heart sound (S3) is present. Respiratory: Fine bibasal crackles evident on deep inspiration. Extremities: Trace bilateral pitting edema at the ankles. DIAGNOSTIC TETRAD: Pansystolic murmur radiating to axilla, Displaced hyperdynamic apex, Soft S1, Backward systolic jet on echo (pending formal echocardiography).
Environmental Observations: Extra pillows stacked on the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine cardiac medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Mitral Regurgitation (causing decompensated Left Ventricular Failure) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Aortic Stenosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Murmur is pansystolic at the apex radiating to the axilla, rather than an ejection systolic murmur at the base radiating to the carotids.

Mitral Stenosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Murmur is pansystolic blowing rather than a low-pitched mid-diastolic rumble, and the apex beat is hyperdynamic rather than tapping.

Primary Pulmonary Disease (COPD/Asthma) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a massive structural heart murmur, displaced apex, S3 gallop, and crackles rather than wheeze.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits classic signs of severe symptomatic mitral regurgitation: a prominent pansystolic apical murmur radiating to the axilla, a hyperdynamic displaced apex beat, and an S3 gallop. The new onset of orthopnea and bibasal crackles indicates that the chronic volume overload has now resulted in left ventricular decompensation and early pulmonary edema. Because the patient is actively deteriorating respiratory-wise, an urgent home physical examination was required to confirm the structural etiology, administer immediate diuretic therapy, and refer for urgent surgical evaluation, safely bypassing the delay of routine in-hours care.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Furosemide 40mg stat to address mild pulmonary congestion. Ordered urgent outpatient echocardiogram and cardiology referral for assessment of valve repair/replacement. Ordered basic metabolic panel and BNP.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instructed the patient to sleep with the head of the bed elevated and to strictly weigh themselves every morning. By tracking weight daily, the patient directly monitors fluid retention caused by the leaky valve. The reward is early identification of fluid overload, allowing for prompt medication adjustment and avoiding future suffocating breathing episodes.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: Advised continued use of a wedge pillow or 3 pillows for sleep.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48 hours to assess diuretic response and review echocardiogram planning.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If breathing suddenly worsens at rest, if coughing produces pink frothy sputum, or if chest pain develops, call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis and severity grading requires definitive ultrasound imaging of the heart (echocardiogram); conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: A severely leaky valve eventually tires out the heart muscle. Once symptoms like breathlessness start, surgical repair or replacement is usually required to restore normal lifespan.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Rest entirely with your head elevated. Seek emergency medical care if you struggle for breath while sitting completely still.

